We see that $\frac{y}{x}=\tan{\left(\frac{\pi}{6}\right)}=\frac{\sqrt{3}}{3}$, so $x=\sqrt{3}y=30$. Thus, $AD = 2y + x = 2y + 30 = 40$. Similarly, $\frac{z}{y}=\tan{\left(\frac{\pi}{6}\right)}=\frac{\sqrt{3}}{3}$, so $y=\sqrt{3}z=40\sqrt{3}$. Thus, $BD = 2z + y = 2z + 40\sqrt{3} = 60\sqrt{3}$. Let $\theta=\angle BAD$. Since $\sin{\angle DAC}=\frac{y-z}{2y}=\frac{40\sqrt{3}-z}{80\sqrt{3}}$ and $\sin{\angle BAD}=\frac{y-z}{40\sqrt{3}}=\frac{40\sqrt{3}-z}{40\sqrt{3}}$, the sine of $\angle DAC$ and $\angle BAD$ is equal to the sine of $\angle BAC$, so $\angle BAD=\angle DAC=\angle BAC$. Therefore, $\theta=\frac{180-90}{3}=30^\circ$. By the Law of Sines, $\frac{\sin{60}}{BD}=\frac{\sin{30}}{CD}$. Substituting in $BD=60\sqrt{3}$, we get $\frac{\sin{60}}{60\sqrt{3}}=\frac{1}{CD}$, or $CD=\frac{60\sqrt{3}}{2}=30\sqrt{3}$. Finally, $AC=AD-CD=2y-(30\sqrt{3})=40-x=10$. Thus, $a+b+c=\boxed{041}$.

The answer is: $041$

